                <h1 class="content-title">Enterprise Governance: <span class="gradient-text">Security, Roles &amp; Update Sets</span></h1>
                <p class="content-subtitle">
                    Learn how enterprise permissions are enforced through Access Control Lists (ACLs) and how developers 
                    safely package and migrate tested code from DEV to PROD using Update Sets.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 1: User Governance -->
            <section class="content-section" id="user-governance">
                <h2>Users, Groups &amp; Roles (The Governance Pyramid)</h2>
                <p>
                    Enterprise security in ServiceNow is structured across three core entities:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>1. Users (`sys_user`)</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Individual employee accounts. Every user has a unique username, email, department, manager, and login credentials.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>2. Groups (`sys_user_group`)</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Collections of users performing similar business duties (e.g. <em>Network Engineering</em>, <em>Service Desk Tier 1</em>, <em>HR Fulfillers</em>).
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>3. Roles (`sys_user_role`)</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Personas that grant specific permissions to access applications, read tables, and execute scripts (e.g. <code>itil</code>, <code>admin</code>, <code>approver_user</code>).
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                    <div class="info-card-title">The Golden Rule of ServiceNow Administration</div>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Always assign roles to Groups, NEVER directly to individual Users!</strong>
                        <br>When an employee joins or switches teams, simply adding them to the appropriate Group automatically grants 
                        them the exact permissions needed. When they leave, removing them from the Group revokes all access instantly.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 2: ACLs -->
            <section class="content-section" id="acls-explained">
                <h2>Access Control Lists (ACLs: The Platform Bouncers)</h2>
                <p>
                    An <strong>Access Control List (ACL)</strong> is a security rule stored in <code>sys_security_acl</code> that 
                    evaluates whether a user is allowed to perform a specific operation on a table or individual field.
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>The 4 CRUD Operations</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            • <code>read</code>: Can the user view this record or field?
                            <br>• <code>write</code>: Can the user edit/update this data?
                            <br>• <code>create</code>: Can the user insert a new record?
                            <br>• <code>delete</code>: Can the user permanently delete this row?
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>The 3-Stage Security Check</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            To pass an ACL, the user must satisfy all 3 criteria:
                            <br>1. <strong>Role:</strong> User has the required role (e.g. <code>itil</code>).
                            <br>2. <strong>Condition:</strong> The record meets filter conditions (e.g. <code>active=true</code>).
                            <br>3. <strong>Script:</strong> Advanced script returns <code>answer = true</code>.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-block">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ACL Security Evaluation Pipeline</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    graph TD
                        REQ["User attempts to View/Edit Field (e.g. incident.work_notes)"] --> ROL{"1. Does User have required Role?"}
                        ROL -->|No| DENY["Access Denied: Field is Hidden / Read-Only"]
                        ROL -->|Yes| COND{"2. Does Record pass Condition Filter?"}
                        
                        COND -->|No| DENY
                        COND -->|Yes| SCR{"3. Does Advanced Script evaluate to true?"}
                        
                        SCR -->|No| DENY
                        SCR -->|Yes| ALLOW["Access Granted: Full Read/Write Permitted"]
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 3: Elevated Privileges -->
            <section class="content-section" id="elevated-privileges">
                <h2>Elevated Privileges &amp; The `security_admin` Role</h2>
                <p>
                    To prevent accidental modifications to security rules, ServiceNow implements <strong>Elevated Privileges</strong>:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Why Standard Admins Can't Edit ACLs</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Even if you are logged in as an <code>admin</code>, you cannot modify Access Control rules by default. 
                            This protects the system from accidental security breaches.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>How to Elevate</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Click your user profile avatar in the top right $\rightarrow$ select <strong>Elevate Roles</strong> $\rightarrow$ 
                            check <code>security_admin</code>. The session privilege expires automatically when you log out.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 4: Update Sets -->
            <section class="content-section" id="update-sets">
                <h2>Update Sets (The Developer's Moving Boxes)</h2>
                <p>
                    Developers never build or test code directly in the live Production (<code>PROD</code>) instance. 
                    Instead, they build in <code>DEV</code>, package their work into an <strong>Update Set</strong>, and migrate it through the pipeline:
                </p>

                <div class="diagram-block">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Enterprise 4-Stage Deployment Pipeline</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    graph LR
                        DEV["1. DEV Instance<br>Build Features in Active Update Set"] --> COMP["2. Mark State = Complete<br>Export XML / Push to Repo"]
                        COMP --> TEST["3. TEST / QA Instance<br>Retrieve &amp; Preview (Resolve Collisions)"]
                        TEST --> PROD["4. PROD Instance<br>Commit Update Set during Maintenance Window"]
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="tabs-container">
                    <div class="tabs-header">
                        <button class="tab-btn active" data-tab="tab-captured">What Gets Captured</button>
                        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-not-captured">What is NOT Captured</button>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel active" id="tab-captured">
                        <h3>✅ Configuration Elements (Tracked in Update Sets)</h3>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li>Custom Tables, Columns &amp; Dictionary entries (`sys_dictionary`).</li>
                            <li>Form Layouts &amp; Section arrangements.</li>
                            <li>Business Rules, Client Scripts &amp; Script Includes.</li>
                            <li>UI Policies, UI Actions (Buttons), and Flow Designer Flows.</li>
                            <li>Access Control Lists (ACLs) &amp; System Properties.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-not-captured">
                        <h3>❌ Data Records (NOT Tracked in Update Sets)</h3>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li>Transactional records (Incidents, Problems, Changes, Tasks).</li>
                            <li>User accounts (`sys_user`) &amp; Groups (`sys_user_group`).</li>
                            <li>Configuration Items / Hardware Assets in the CMDB.</li>
                            <li><em>Note:</em> Data must be exported via XML or imported via Import Sets / CSV.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 5: Scoped Apps -->
            <section class="content-section" id="scoped-apps">
                <h2>Application Scopes (Global vs. Scoped Apps)</h2>
                <p>
                    In modern ServiceNow development, custom business solutions are packaged as <strong>Scoped Applications</strong>:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Global Scope (Legacy Shared Area)</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Historically, all scripts and tables lived in the Global scope. If two developers created a field with the 
                            same name, they could accidentally break each other's applications.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Scoped Applications (Sandboxed Containers)</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Every custom app receives its own unique namespace prefix (e.g. <code>x_acme_badge_mgr</code>). 
                            Tables and scripts are sandboxed, preventing other apps from making unauthorized changes to your database.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

